{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Honor your father and your mother.</b> With this commandment He sealed the first five statements, which speak of the honor of the Place, blessed be He, for this reason 'ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ' is stated in all of them, and He did not mention the Name in all five latter statements, which speak of matters between man and his fellow. And the commandment of honoring father and mother — even though it is between man and his fellow, nevertheless this commandment touches also upon the honor of the Place, blessed be He, because three partners are in man: the Holy One, blessed be He, and his father and his mother. And if you honor father and mother because from them the matter and the body — which waste away and perish — were formed, then all the more so, the conclusion of the conclusion of an a fortiori argument, should you honor your Father in heaven, who placed within you the soul, the superior portion that endures forever. And in tractate Kiddushin (30b) the Rabbans taught: 'Three partners are in man' etc., and it concludes there: 'At a time when a person honors his father and his mother, I account it upon you as though I dwelt among you and you honored Me.' And the question arises: dwelling — who mentioned it? And from where did they learn to say 'as though I dwelt among you'? Surely they learned this from what we find: that in all five latter statements the Name was not mentioned, because they speak of matters between man and his fellow — and if so, why did He mention 'ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ' by honoring father and mother? Rather, the Holy One, blessed be He, made a dwelling for His great Name, blessed be He, by father and mother, to say that one who honors father and mother — it is as though 'I dwelt among you and you honored Me.' And therefore his reward is length of days, for cleaving to YHWH, the source of life, grants long life to a person. And if he honors father and mother because from them the matter was formed, then surely also the soul, a divine portion from above, should give honor to her Father in heaven, and through the cleaving that she has with Him she will merit length of days, as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:4) 'וְאַתֶּם הַדְּבֵקִים בַּה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם חַיִּים כֻּלְּכֶם הַיּוֹם'."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:21:48.991164737Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}